205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E ( The war began on August 2, 1990, when Iraqi forces, under Saddam Hussein, invaded and took over the state of Kuwait. This act was immediately condemned by the UN Security Council, leading to the formation of a massive international coalition led by the United States.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (Operation Desert Storm: In January 1991, the coalition launched a military campaign to liberate Kuwait. The war was short but decisive, resulting in the retreat of Iraqi forces by February 1991.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (The Ceasefire Agreement: On April 3, 1991, the UNSC passed Resolution 687. This is the most important document in this committee, as it sets the terms for the end of the war. In that resolution, there were two main terms:)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 420 0 R
/E (Disarmament: Iraq was required to unconditionally accept destroying, removing, or handing over all chemical and biological weapons, as well as ballistic missiles with a range greater than 150km.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 421 0 R
/E (Inspections: Iraq had to agree to urgent, on-site inspections by the UN Special Commission \(UNSCOM\) and the IAEA to verify that these weapons were gone.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (The 1991 Gulf War did not result in regime change. Saddam Hussein remained in power, but Iraq was placed under a strict containment policy. This created years of Tom and Jerry between Iraqi officials and the UN inspectors.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
