>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Jordan: They view the war as a threat to the region. Mostly concerned by a potential refugee crisis and total regional instability.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Iran: They hate Saddam but fear a permanent US presence on their border. They want to exploit and expand their own influence in post-war Iraq.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (India: They refuse to take sides between the US and the anti-war bloc. Their stance is that a war will disrupt global economies and cause chaos in oil prices.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Saudi Arabia: A close ally with the US and want Saddam removed, but they face massive anti-war pressure from the Arab world. They avoid letting the US use their lands to plan an attack. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Japan: Due to their peaceful constitution, they cannot provide troops for a war. They support the US politically.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Canada: They support disarming Iraq, but will not join a war without a second resolution.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Norway: They argue that going to War without UN approval sets a dangerous precedent for global peace.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Libya: They view the political invasion as an illegal western oil grab. They fully stand with Iraq.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
